WEBVTT

NOTE week04-clip1-modern-block
NOTE Narration read by an AI-generated voice.
NOTE The clip draws its own caption along the bottom of the frame. These cues
NOTE sit there too, so that no part of the animation is ever covered.

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:05.634
A large language model in 2026 is
still a stack of decoder blocks,

2
00:00:05.634 --> 00:00:10.940
much like the 2017 transformer:
attention, then a feed-forward network,

3
00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:14.150
each with a residual connection.

4
00:00:14.350 --> 00:00:17.411
The first change is normalization.

5
00:00:17.411 --> 00:00:24.371
It now comes before each sublayer
instead of after, and it uses RMS norm,

6
00:00:24.371 --> 00:00:29.750
which only rescales each
vector by its root mean square.

7
00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:35.804
Second, the feed-forward network becomes
swig-loo. The token is projected twice,

8
00:00:35.804 --> 00:00:39.441
and one projection passes
through a smooth activation

9
00:00:39.441 --> 00:00:42.750
and acts as a gate on the other.

10
00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:46.600
Third, positions are no
longer added at the input.

11
00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:51.770
Instead, RoPE rotates every query
and key inside each attention layer,

12
00:00:51.770 --> 00:00:54.350
as we saw in week two.

13
00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:58.621
Fourth, attention becomes
grouped-query attention.

14
00:00:58.621 --> 00:01:04.180
In SmolLM2, 15 query heads
share 5 key and value heads,

15
00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:08.417
which saves memory during generation.

16
00:01:08.617 --> 00:01:14.987
The overall shape is unchanged.
The 2017 decoder stacked 6 blocks,

17
00:01:14.987 --> 00:01:22.113
SmolLM2 stacks 32, and the last block
feeds the prediction of the next token.
